WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:06.480
What did you come up with? Let’s talk through&nbsp;
this code and find and fix the two problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:16.640
with the code. First, for the print statement on&nbsp;
line 7 to display, the score must be over 10,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.760
and the coin needs to be gold or platinum. The&nbsp;
first problem exists on line 5, and it’s a common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:29.320
problem. The app's logic should dictate that once&nbsp;
someone reaches a score of 10,000, the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:36.400
could be on level 2 or 3. But, the logic on line 5&nbsp;
is only looking for scores over 10,000. The common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.680
problem with this code is that people often say,&nbsp;
“Well, if they are over 10,000, do this,” when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:48.000
they really mean, “If they are at or over 10,000,&nbsp;
do this.” When someone tells you this logic, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.440
may need to clarify if they mean that the amount&nbsp;
they want to use as a condition includes the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:01.160
amount. In many cases, the condition will include&nbsp;
the exact amount. Thus, we want to change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:10.080
comparison on line 5 to greater than or equal.&nbsp;
I will make that change and rerun the code. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:19.600
result is different, but note that we do not quite&nbsp;
get the result we are looking for. We are about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.920
see the example I said you would need to think&nbsp;
through, at least possibly. The coin is gold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:32.440
as we see on line 2, but look at the casing here&nbsp;
compared to the if statement on line 6. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:38.360
the word is the same, Python is treating these&nbsp;
as two different values because one is lowercase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:45.200
and the other is title case. The lesson here is&nbsp;
that Python criteria are case-sensitive. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:54.240
I will change the criterion on line 2 to&nbsp;
capital G Gold and rerun the code. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:01.520
the code works as intended. Get practice analyzing&nbsp;
code line by line, as learning how to analyze code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.120
will help you for the exam and help you greatly&nbsp;
in figuring out and troubleshooting code.

